  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 126 PM EDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\n- Dry and mild through the weekend\n\nThe blocking ridge over Ontario/Quebec continues to dominate our\nweather and will through the weekend. Latest visible loop shows\nclear skies over much of the cwa with some diurnal co over the\nsouthern row...around the periphery of the Ohio Valley low. We\naren't going to see much change for several more days as the\nblocking pattern remains in place. Highs from the mid 60s to lower\n70s Friday will gradually warm to highs in the lower to mid 70s by\nMonday. Lows in the mid to upper 40s are expected each day. \n\n- Chance of rain late Tuesday/Wednesday\n\nThe GFS has taken a turn toward the ECMWF late Tuesday and\nWednesday and now shows a northern stream wave moving across the\nupper Midwest and knocking down the ridge. Concurrently, a\nsouthern stream wave helps to develop a surface low in the\nsouthern Plains. This result is that the cold front pushed across\nthe state by the northern wave gets hung up over the cwa late\nTuesday into Wednesday by the developing Plains low. This frontal\nboundary then becomes quasi stationary and a focus for shower\ndevelopment. Thus, confidence is fairly high that we'll see\nprecipitation late Tuesday into Wednesday that may linger into\nThursday. ECMWF and GFS ensembles also show this. At this point,\nthe thunder threat looks low given weak instability.",
